To set up a React app with Azure B2C authentication, a .NET Core API for token verification, and logging, follow these steps:


---

1. Set Up Azure AD B2C

Step 1: Create an Azure AD B2C Tenant

1. Go to the Azure Portal.


2. Search for Azure AD B2C and create a tenant (if not already created).


3. Link the B2C tenant to your Azure subscription.



Step 2: Register the Web API

1. Azure AD B2C → App registrations → New registration.


2. Name: MyB2CApi


3. Supported account types: Accounts in any identity provider.


4. Redirect URI: Leave empty (API doesn’t require a redirect).


5. Click Register and copy the Application (client) ID.



Step 3: Define API Scope

1. Open the Web API registration.


2. Navigate to Expose an API → Set Application ID URI (e.g., https://yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com/api).


3. Click Add a scope:

Scope name: access_api

Admin consent: Allows access to My API

Click Save.




Step 4: Register the React App

1. Azure AD B2C → App registrations → New registration.


2. Name: MyReactApp


3. Platform: SPA (Single Page Application).


4. Redirect URI: http://localhost:3000


5. API Permissions → Add permission:

My APIs → Select MyB2CApi → access_api scope.

Click Grant admin consent.




Step 5: Create User Flow (Policy)

1. Azure AD B2C → User flows → New user flow.


2. Choose Sign up and sign in.


3. Select Email as an identity provider.


4. Choose Claims (Name, Email).


5. Click Create and copy the policy name (e.g., B2C_1A_SIGNUP_SIGNIN).




---

2. Set Up React App

Step 1: Create React App

npx create-react-app my-app
cd my-app
npm install @azure/msal-browser @azure/msal-react react-router-dom

Step 2: Configure MSAL

Create src/authConfig.js:

import { PublicClientApplication } from "@azure/msal-browser";

const msalConfig = {
  auth: {
    clientId: "YOUR_REACT_APP_CLIENT_ID",
    authority: "https://YOUR_TENANT_NAME.b2clogin.com/YOUR_TENANT_NAME.onmicrosoft.com/B2C_1A_SIGNUP_SIGNIN",
    knownAuthorities: ["YOUR_TENANT_NAME.b2clogin.com"],
    redirectUri: "http://localhost:3000",
  },
};

export const msalInstance = new PublicClientApplication(msalConfig);

Step 3: Wrap App with MSAL Provider

Edit src/index.js:

import React from "react";
import ReactDOM from "react-dom";
import { MsalProvider } from "@azure/msal-react";
import { msalInstance } from "./authConfig";
import App from "./App";

ReactDOM.render(
  <MsalProvider instance={msalInstance}>
    <App />
  </MsalProvider>,
  document.getElementById("root")
);

Step 4: Implement Login

Create src/components/AuthButton.js:

import React from "react";
import { useMsal } from "@azure/msal-react";

const AuthButton = () => {
  const { instance, accounts } = useMsal();

  const handleLogin = () => {
    instance.loginPopup().catch(error => console.error(error));
  };

  const handleLogout = () => {
    instance.logoutPopup().catch(error => console.error(error));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {accounts.length > 0 ? (
        <button onClick={handleLogout}>Logout</button>
      ) : (
        <button onClick={handleLogin}>Login</button>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default AuthButton;

Step 5: Get Token & Call API

Create src/api.js:

import { useMsal } from "@azure/msal-react";

export const useAuthToken = () => {
  const { instance } = useMsal();

  const getToken = async () => {
    const accounts = instance.getAllAccounts();
    if (accounts.length === 0) return null;

    const request = {
      scopes: ["https://yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com/api/access_api"],
      account: accounts[0],
    };

    try {
      const response = await instance.acquireTokenSilent(request);
      return response.accessToken;
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("Token error:", error);
      return null;
    }
  };

  return { getToken };
};


---

3. Set Up ASP.NET Core API

Step 1: Create API

dotnet new webapi -n MyB2CApi
cd MyB2CApi

Step 2: Install Dependencies

dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer
dotnet add package Serilog.AspNetCore

Step 3: Configure Authentication (Program.cs)

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;
using Microsoft.Identity.Web;
using Serilog;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Add logging
Log.Logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .WriteTo.Console()
    .WriteTo.File("logs/log-.txt", rollingInterval: RollingInterval.Day)
    .CreateLogger();

builder.Host.UseSerilog();

// Configure authentication
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(builder.Configuration.GetSection("AzureAdB2C"));

builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
builder.Services.AddControllers();

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseSerilogRequestLogging();
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();

app.Run();

Step 4: Configure App Settings

Edit appsettings.json:

{
  "AzureAdB2C": {
    "Instance": "https://yourtenant.b2clogin.com",
    "ClientId": "YOUR_API_CLIENT_ID",
    "Domain": "yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com",
    "SignUpSignInPolicyId": "B2C_1A_SIGNUP_SIGNIN"
  },
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning"
    }
  }
}

Step 5: Protect API Endpoint

Create Controllers/SecureController.cs:

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

[Authorize]
[ApiController]
[Route("[controller]")]
public class SecureController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly ILogger<SecureController> _logger;

    public SecureController(ILogger<SecureController> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult Get()
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Protected API accessed successfully.");
        return Ok(new { message = "Secure data accessed." });
    }
}


---

4. Test Logging and Authentication

Start API

dotnet run

Logs are stored in logs/log-<date>.txt.


Start React App

npm start

Open DevTools Console to check token errors.


Call API with Token

Edit src/components/ApiComponent.js:

import React, { useState } from "react";
import { useAuthToken } from "../api";

const ApiComponent = () => {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  const { getToken } = useAuthToken();

  const callApi = async () => {
    const token = await getToken();
    if (!token) return;

    const response = await fetch("https://localhost:5001/Secure", {
      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
    });

    const result = await response.json();
    setData(result);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={callApi}>Call API</button>
      {data && <pre>{JSON.stringify(data, null, 2)}</pre>}
    </div>
  );
};

export default ApiComponent;


